{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>And when a man sells his daughter.</b> And not a woman her daughter." "<b>His daughter.</b> And not his son." "<b>She shall not go out.</b> כצאת העבדים — like the going out of Canaanite slaves, etc. Or is it only like the going out of Hebrew slaves, at six years and at the Jubilee? Scripture says: ״כי ימכר לך אחיך העברי או העבריה״ — it equates the Hebrew woman to the Hebrew man for all her goings out, etc. And if you ask: just as we equate the Hebrew woman to the Hebrew man for going out at six years and the Jubilee, why do we not equate the Hebrew man to the Hebrew woman for going out by signs (סימנים)? For we hold: there is no such equation halfway — meaning he too would go out by signs, like the Hebrew maidservant. But you cannot say he goes out by signs, for in this manner how do you find it? If he sold himself when he was a minor — a minor's act is nothing, and his father too is not permitted to sell him, as said above. And if he sold himself when he was an adult — he has already brought signs. And further: it equates the Hebrew man to the Hebrew woman — just as she does not go out by the loss of initial limbs (ראשי אברים), so too he does not go out by the loss of initial limbs. And if you ask: why do I need a verse that he does not go out by initial limbs? Let it be derived by an a fortiori (ק״ו): if the Hebrew woman, who goes out by signs, does not go out by initial limbs, then the Hebrew man, who does not go out by signs — is it not logical that he should not go out by initial limbs? One may answer: this a fortiori has a refutation — what about the Hebrew woman, who does not go out by initial limbs, that is because she is not sold for her theft; you might say this of a Hebrew slave who is sold for his theft — therefore Scripture says ״העברי או העבריה״, equating the Hebrew man to the Hebrew woman, etc.\n\n<b>She shall not go out like the going out of the slaves.</b> According to its plain sense, the Torah teaches proper conduct: that she should not be sent about like the slave, whose master dispatches him on his errands by day and by night, in the city and outside the city — all this is disgraceful for a woman; only service within the house, because ״כל כבודה בת מלך פנימה״, and also she is a minor.\n\nAnother interpretation: <b>she shall not go out like the going out of the slaves</b> — who go out without children, as we said above. But this one's going out is not so, for if you wish to keep her, you yourself may marry her, and she and her children will be yours from then on; and she shall not go out from you except by a bill of divorce and a ketubah, like other daughters."], :book :exodus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:59:20.958349513Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}